## How work is dispatched

**One queue, not one per channel.** Both entry points — creating a campaign and creating a single
application — call the same `enqueueOutreach(payload)` with a `channel` field on the message body. A
single dispatcher process long-polls that queue and routes each message to a channel handler. Adding
email meant adding a handler and a switch case, not a queue, a worker, an IAM policy and a deploy
target.

**The queue absorbs the difference in channel physics.** Plivo rate-limits outbound calls, so the
voice handler walks its candidate list with a 10-second gap. WhatsApp has no such limit, so its
handler fans out concurrently with `Promise.allSettled`. Both look identical to the producer:
enqueue a batch, return. The recruiter's HTTP request never waits on a phone call or a Meta API
round trip.

**Deletion is a decision, not a default.** Every handler returns `{ success, deleteMessage, reason }`,
and the dispatcher deletes the message only when told to. That distinction is the whole retry policy:

- A **permanent** failure — a 4xx from Meta, a malformed job description — is deleted. Retrying
  produces the same failure and burns quota.
- A **transient** failure — a timeout, a network blip — is left alone. The visibility timeout
  expires and the message reappears for another attempt.
- After **3 failed receives**, the redrive policy moves it to a dead-letter queue, where it can be
  inspected rather than lost.

Long polling (20s wait, up to 10 messages per receive) keeps the dispatcher near-idle between
campaigns instead of hammering the API on an interval.

---

## How duplicate work is prevented

Retries are the price of the design above, and retries mean the same job arrives more than once.
Three sources: SQS at-least-once delivery, a worker crashing mid-job and its message reappearing,
and Meta re-sending webhooks aggressively until it gets a 200.

**A Postgres table is the lock.** `processing_jobs` has a unique constraint on `idempotency_key`,
and acquiring a lock is a single atomic statement:

```sql
INSERT INTO processing_jobs (idempotency_key, status, worker_id, expires_at, ...)
VALUES (...) ON CONFLICT (idempotency_key) DO NOTHING
```

The insert either wins or it doesn't. No read-then-write race, no separate lock service.

**Locks are leases, not flags.** Each holds an `expires_at` (5 minutes by default), so a worker that
dies mid-job doesn't block that job forever — the next worker sees an expired lease and takes it
over, incrementing `attempt_count`. Long-running jobs call `extendLock` to keep the lease alive
rather than being reclaimed while still working. Acquisition returns one of five outcomes —
`new_lock`, `already_completed`, `retry_failed`, `stale_lock_takeover`, `lock_held_by_other` — and
the caller acts on the distinction instead of a bare boolean.

**Completed and failed are treated differently on purpose.** `COMPLETED` means skip: the work is
done and re-running it would double-send. `FAILED` means take over and retry, because a job that
failed on a transient error should not be permanently poisoned by its own failure record.

**Keys name the unit of work, not the message.**

| Job | Key |
|---|---|
| Outreach send | `outreach:{channel}:{applicationCallId}` |
| Resume scoring | `resume:{resumeId}:app:{applicationId}:job:{jobPostId}` |
| Inbound WhatsApp reply | `whatsapp:inbound:{phoneNumberId}:{messageId}` |

Because the key is derived from domain identifiers, two different SQS messages describing the same
work collapse onto the same lock.

**The webhook path leans on this hardest.** Meta retries until it gets a 200, so the endpoint returns
200 immediately and processes the message fire-and-forget. Every duplicate delivery resolves to the
same inbound key and is dropped at the lock. Admin re-score is the deliberate escape hatch:
`clearLock` deletes the row so the pipeline will genuinely reprocess a completed job. Completed locks
older than 7 days are swept by a cleanup job.

SQS FIFO's built-in deduplication wasn't sufficient here — its 5-minute window is producer-side only,
and the duplicates that actually matter come from consumer crashes and webhook retries across process
restarts.

